      "secondarySkills": [
        "productivity_calculation"
      ],
      "repairSkill": "real_gdp_per_person",
      "commonError": "Comparing total GDP instead of output per person.",
      "q": "Two economies have equally scarce capital and similar access to technology. One has reliable electricity and trained operators; the other has frequent outages and little training. Which evidence best limits a claim that both must catch up equally fast?",
      "options": [
        "The complementary inputs differ, so equal capital scarcity does not imply equal realized gains",
        "Scarce capital has a low marginal product in both, regardless of complementary inputs",
        "Technology access proves training and infrastructure no longer matter",
        "A country’s current income ranking fixes its future growth ranking"
      ],
      "tag": "growth_differences",
      "type": "interpretation",
      "objective": "LO26.1",
      "feedback": "Capital scarcity can create high-return opportunities, but skilled workers and usable infrastructure help realize them. Catch-up is conditional; equal starting capital alone does not establish equal productivity growth.",
